menu "Launcher"

    choice LAUNCHER_BUILD_TYPE
        prompt "Build type"
        default LAUNCHER_RELEASE
        help
            What this build is for. Exactly one of these is ever true, so
            code can check CONFIG_LAUNCHER_DEVELOPMENT and know it means
            "not a release build" - not "development, or maybe something
            else nobody has named yet."

        config LAUNCHER_RELEASE
            bool "Release"
            help
                The product, and nothing else. No test suites, no profiling
                counters, no logging meant to be read over a serial console.
                This project does not do telemetry - nobody downstream is
                ever reading those numbers - so anything a developer alone
                would want is pure cost here: flash for the strings, cycles
                for the bookkeeping, for output that helps nobody. None of
                it ships in this build.

        config LAUNCHER_DEVELOPMENT
            bool "Development"
            help
                Turns on everything meant for someone AT the device or
                watching its serial console while working on it - frame
                timing, step counters, that kind of thing. Guard code with
                CONFIG_LAUNCHER_DEVELOPMENT, not CONFIG_LAUNCHER_SELFTEST,
                unless what it is actually gating is the test suites
                themselves - see LAUNCHER_SELFTEST below, which requires
                this rather than turning it on for you. Turn this on by itself for a build that wants
                the profiling and logging without the test suites too.

    endchoice

    config LAUNCHER_SELFTEST
        bool "Include the on-device test suites"
        default n
        depends on LAUNCHER_DEVELOPMENT
        help
            Compiles the test suites into the firmware and runs them at boot,
            before the shell starts.

            Off by default, so a normal build carries no test code at all.
            The suites draw to the framebuffer and drive the panel, which is
            harmless in a diagnostics build and unacceptable in a shipped one,
            and test hooks in a release image are a liability rather than a
            feature.

            Turn this on through the diagnostics build rather than by hand:

                ./test/run_device_tests.sh

            which builds this variant into its own directory, flashes it,
            collects the results over the serial console and exits non-zero if
            anything failed.

    config LAUNCHER_SELFTEST_AUTORUN
        bool "Run the on-device test suites automatically at boot"
        depends on LAUNCHER_SELFTEST
        default n
        help
            LAUNCHER_SELFTEST above compiles the suites in; this decides
            whether they also RUN automatically at boot, versus sitting
            compiled-in until triggered on demand from the Diagnostics app's
            toggle screen.

            Off by default. The suite count has grown well past the "roughly
            half a second" boot/selftest.c's own comment once described, so
            paying that cost on every boot of an interactive diagnostics
            session - just to check the POST report or flip a toggle - makes
            the build too slow to use for anything else. Turn this on only
            for an automated harness that needs the suites to run themselves,
            such as tools/report_test_results.sh, which layers
            sdkconfig.defaults.diag_autorun on top of sdkconfig.defaults.diag
            for exactly that reason.

    config LAUNCHER_SAND_PASS_GATES
        bool "Sand: per-pass volatile gates for sand_step()"
        default n
        depends on LAUNCHER_DEVELOPMENT
        help
            Compiles in four volatile bools (sand_priv.h) that let a probe
            disable one of sand_step()'s four passes - main sweep,
            cross-flow, gas, reactions - at a time without a rebuild.
            Default true at runtime (every pass on), so behaviour is
            untouched until a probe flips one off.

            This buys a device pass decomposition - which pass owns a
            scene's cost - as one binary, five configurations (all passes
            on, then each disabled in turn), and one capture, with no
            layout difference between configurations to confound the
            comparison. Four separately-built stub-each-pass images would
            each draw their own flash-layout ticket instead.

            Deliberately NOT paired with the sand work counters, which live
            in tools/perf_probe/ and are injected into a scratch build by
            compare_counters.py rather than compiled from this tree. The
            gates measure TIME on device; the counters cost sand_step_liquids
            +39 instructions and +120 bytes, measured against the real
            toolchain, so building both together would perturb exactly the
            timings the gates exist to produce. Off by default because
            build.diag is what every frame-budget capture is
            taken on, and this campaign chases effects too small to risk
            shifting by default.

endmenu
